A||||18||batboy||Batmobile won't start||||1||
Z||000000||batboy||06-08-2000||11:34 PM||||If it weren't for bad luck, I'd have no luck at all. After work, I stopped at the post office three blocks away to check mail. When I hopped back in, the car was dead...nothing except a faint click. The volt meter showed low voltage, but lights and power windows all work ok. Jump starting was useless. I put a known good battery in, still nothing. I charged up the old battery, still nothing. Good thing I was close to home...dragged it home with a chain...embarassing! Didn't do anything else to it tonight. I got my 85 GT back together instead so I had a vehicle to drive to work tomorrow. Didn't cuss or swear tonight, maybe I got it all out of my system last night, but I'm very disappointed with the Batmobile...I was beginning to trust it. I had put over 1000 miles on it without any problems, which is a record. Oh guano!||||reg||1
Z||000001||william_thorniii||06-08-2000||11:39 PM||||Clutch Pedal?<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||5
Z||000002||BAM-BAM||06-08-2000||11:50 PM||||   BB, Maybe a weak starter solenoid? Just went through that with my 85GT. When it would warm up the solenoid wouldn't engage. After it cooled down, VROOM! Changed out the starter, all is good again <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Check it out.<P> Late, BAM OUT!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||12
Z||000003||86SE||06-09-2000||12:10 AM||||Yep, sounds just like a starter problem.  Had the SAME problem with a Dodge van, put a new starter in and it fired right up.||||reg||7
Z||000004||DiggityDan||06-09-2000||01:23 AM||||Try tapping on the side of the starter a few times.sometimes the gears get stuck when they engage and won't turn the flywheel, thus the click, and nothing else. Also try grounds||||reg||1
Z||000005||dogtired||06-09-2000||02:03 AM||||DiggityDan:  I think that is the BFH that MonkeyMan has told us about earlier this year?    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">     Come to think about it? It might have been batboy that told us that?? The mind is a terrible thing to waste? I just can't remember  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif"><BR>If all your lights and windows are strong, I would have to agree with the starter??? <BR>Are you aware that a 4cyl starter will bolt right up and will work just fine??? Just another option you might have handy. Did that on the 87SE, added an extra shimm but it works. Just passing on info!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000006||Standard||06-09-2000||04:06 AM||||Check the fusible links going to and the connections at the starter.  <p>[This message has been edited by Standard (edited 06-09-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Mattsfiero||06-09-2000||04:27 AM||||I agree with the starter trouble. I had a simular problem recently. Check your positive cable attachment to your starter. Look for cracks in the housing where it attaches. I am willing to bet you will find the problem there.||||reg||12
Z||000008||batboy||06-09-2000||06:51 AM||||The grounds are all making good connections. I was thinking the starter was heat soaked, but it still won't turn over even after sitting all night. Tomorrow I'll have to crawl under it with a multi-tester and check the starter wires. I'm hoping it's not the starter, because it's an expensive high torque Tilton unit. The long-time Forum members may recall that the wiring was butchered up pretty bad when I first got this car and I spent lots of time last summer fixing and rerouting wires. So, it could be a wire problem. The starter was never given me a lick of trouble yet. But, I might have to do the love tap thing and/or pull it and have it tested. I'm just glad it didn't leave me stranded in the middle of nowhere. Well...ok, I live in the middle of nowhere, but at least I was only three blocks from home.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Monkeyman||06-09-2000||10:31 AM||||dog--If it makes any difference, the BFH can be attributed to WT III.  Catchy, isn't it? <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000010||batboy||06-09-2000||12:02 PM||||Billy the third may of used it first on the Forum, but I heard that used first in 1975 when I was in the military, along with BFR (which are large fornicating boulders).||||reg||1
Z||000011||Formula||06-09-2000||05:22 PM||||also sounds like the neutral start switch.  At least make sure that its still plugged in before ripping the starter out.||||reg||1
Z||000012||batboy||06-09-2000||05:58 PM||||I wondered about that too, I wiggled the shifter around in park and in neutral while turning the key and still nothing. I hear a faint click which means it's sending some voltage to the solenoid. I suppose I'd better jumper the switch just to eliminate that possibility. I'm really beginning to suspect a loose or shorted wire, probably from the switch to the solenoid. I hate troubleshooting electrical problems!||||reg||1
Z||000013||lowCG||06-09-2000||09:04 PM||||Did you try shorting the connections at the starter yet?<BR>Might even be the switch.(?)||||reg||1
Z||000014||batboy||06-09-2000||09:12 PM||||The Batmobile is back on the road! YIPPEE! First prize for the closest guess would have to go to Standard because he said to check the connections at the starter. I will take a close second place because I decided it was a wire problem from the switch to the solenoid. Those who said starter problem get honorable mention. Even though it technically wasn't the starter, once you slid under it, you would of noticed that the solenoid wire had fell off and was dangling. Installed a new connector and I'm back in business.||||reg||1
Z||000015||batboy||06-10-2000||10:24 AM||||Did I mention the Batmobile is back on the road and didn't cost anything to fix?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000016||Standard||06-11-2000||03:31 AM||||Sweet!  So what would first prize be?  <P>Actually, I had the same problem with my 84 SC... one of the fusible links was gone and the connections weren't too good at the starter, either.||||reg||1
